        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "thinking",
            "text": "The parser panics on empty input. Read `src/lib.rs` first, then make `parse` return an `Option` so callers can show an empty state.",
            "redacted": false
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "## Plan\n\n`parse` calls `Config::from` without checking for empty input. I will:\n\n1. Return `Option<Config>` from `parse`.\n2. Add a regression test for blank input.\n\n```rust\npub fn parse(input: &str) -> Option<Config> {\n    if input.trim().is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    Some(Config::from(input))\n}\n```\n\nThe TypeScript caller only needs a null check:\n\n```ts\nconst config = parse(text);\nif (config === null) showEmptyState();\n```\n\n| File | Change |\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/lib.rs` | `parse` returns `Option` |\n| `ui/app.ts` | handles `null` |\n\nBackground: [Option in the Rust book](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch06-01-defining-an-enum.html).\n\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "toolCall",
            "id": "call-1",
            "name": "read",
            "args": {
              "path": "src/lib.rs"
            },
            "intent": "Read the parser"
          },
          {
